{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n \\\"Invocation log entry|### .* — .*@.*|branch-protection-log\\\" docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024 -g '\"'!**/*.log'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-completeness-r4.json:107:        \"text\": \"PR MUST NOT modify docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md (no enable-required-check.sh invocation occurred — closing the bypass surface that R3 safety surfaced) AND MUST reference the filed TF-024X-conflict-<adopter> TF\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-completeness-r4.json:111:        \"text\": \"the PR MUST NOT modify branch-protection-log.md (CI script asserts absence of modification per invariant 2)\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-completeness-r4.json:129:      \"evidence\": \"Plan-doc line 208 (§7 risk surface, R-024-06 mitigation column): 'T-024-10 mitigation: cascade slice CLOSES with status blocked-on-adopter-conflict per invariant 10 (forward TF TF-024X-conflict-<adopter> owned by the slice author; new sub-slice opens on conflict resolution). The CI invocation-log check (invariant 2 carve-out) skips log-entry assertion for these close-state slices but verifies the TF reference is present.' The phrase 'skips log-entry assertion for these close-state slices but verifies the TF reference is present' describes a passive check (the CI skip the positive assertion that a log entry exists, and adds one positive TF reference check). This directly contradicts the R3 safety fix applied in fix-round-3 to three other locations: (1) invariant 2 line 47: 'PR MUST NOT modify docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md ... AND MUST reference the filed TF-024X-conflict-<adopter> TF'; (2) §5.2 line 119: 'the PR MUST NOT modify branch-protection-log.md (CI script asserts absence of modification per invariant 2)'; (3) §6 024B acceptance row line 190: 'for blocked-on-adopter-conflict: requires TF-024X-conflict-<adopter> reference AND asserts log file was NOT modified (closes the R3 safety bypass)'. The R3 safety finding (FIX-ROUND-3.md R3-NEW-MAJ-001 safety) was precisely that the prior version of invariant 2 only required positive evidence (TF reference present) but did NOT require negative evidence (log NOT modified). R-024-06 still describes the old pre-R3 posture: one positive check ('verifies the TF reference is present'), no negative assertion. The R3 safety fix added the second requirement: the CI MUST assert log file was NOT modified. R-024-06 omits this second requirement entirely. R-024-06 is normative governance text — it is the risk-surface summary that a future cascade slice author (024C–024F) reads to understand what the CI gate enforces for conflict-close slices. The stale 'skips log-entry assertion' framing could cause a slice author to believe a passive omission satisfies the CI gate, which would reopen the bypass surface that R3 safety closed.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-completeness-r4.json:130:      \"recommendation\": \"Update plan-doc §7 R-024-06 mitigation column (line 208) to replace 'The CI invocation-log check (invariant 2 carve-out) skips log-entry assertion for these close-state slices but verifies the TF reference is present.' with 'The CI enforcement script (024B) applies the blocked-on-adopter-conflict check-path per invariant 2: the PR MUST NOT modify docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md (CI asserts absence of modification — no invocation occurred) AND MUST reference the filed TF-024X-conflict-<adopter> in DISPATCH-NOTE. (Closes 024A R3 R3-NEW-MAJ-001 safety — earlier text described only the positive TF check, omitting the negative log-file assertion.)'\"\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-completeness-r5.json:14:      \"evidence\": \"Plan-doc line 208 (§7 risk surface, R-024-06 mitigation column). The old text 'The CI invocation-log check (invariant 2 carve-out) skips log-entry assertion for these close-state slices but verifies the TF reference is present' has been replaced with: 'CI enforcement (invariant 2): when cascade-status: blocked-on-adopter-conflict, check-invocation-log-entry.sh (a) verifies the TF-024X-conflict-<adopter> reference IS present in DISPATCH-NOTE AND (b) actively asserts that docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md was NOT modified in the PR diff (closes the R3 safety bypass — a slice declaring conflict-close while actually invoking the script must fail CI). Both assertions are required; one positive + one negative. Closes 024A R4 R4-NEW-MAJ-001 (R-024-06 stale language flagged by all 3 lenses).' Both assertions are now named; the stale 'skips log-entry assertion' framing is gone; the closure marker is present at the correct site. The fix matches FIX-ROUND-4.md's stated action for R4-NEW-MAJ-001 ('Rewrote R-024-06 mitigation with both positive (TF reference present) AND negative (log file NOT modified) assertions explicitly named'). R4-NEW-MAJ-001 is CLOSED.\"\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-completeness-r5.json:21:      \"evidence\": \"Plan-doc line 73 (§3 programme protocol position). The text now reads: 'each adopter cascade slice is a single PR on the SCP repo shipping (a) the cascade slice's DISPATCH-NOTE with a cascade-status: field declaring one of three close-states per §5.2 + invariant 2, (b) the invocation-log entry under docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md only when cascade-status is onboarded or onboarded-operator-bump (when cascade-status: blocked-on-adopter-conflict, no log entry is added — the CI asserts log-file was NOT modified per invariant 2), (c) any SCP-side ADOPT-001 / scaffolder amendments... Closes 024A R4 R4-NEW-MAJ-002 (correctness — §3 unconditional invocation-log claim contradicted invariant 2's conditional carve-out).' The item (b) is now explicitly conditional on cascade-status; the blocked-on-adopter-conflict no-log-entry carve-out is named in-line; the correctness-qualified closure marker is present. The fix matches FIX-ROUND-4.md's stated action ('§3 line 73 reworded to make item (b) conditional on cascade-status being onboarded or onboarded-operator-bump; conflict-close path explicitly noted as no-log-entry'). R4-NEW-MAJ-002 (correctness) is CLOSED.\"\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-completeness-r5.json:139:      \"evidence\": \"Plan-doc line 119 (§5.2 item (2), blocked-on-adopter-conflict value definition): 'blocked-on-adopter-conflict — invariant-10 conflict-close path; NO invocation occurred (conflict was discovered before invocation); a TF-024X-conflict-<adopter> TF is filed + referenced in DISPATCH-NOTE; the PR MUST NOT modify branch-protection-log.md (CI script asserts absence of modification per invariant 2).' The TF reference requirement reads 'filed + referenced in DISPATCH-NOTE' — no meaningful-content qualifier. Fix-round-4 added the meaningful-content requirement to invariant 2 line 47: 'MUST reference the filed TF-024X-conflict-<adopter> TF in DISPATCH-NOTE matching the standardised prefix with meaningful content (status field + non-empty description; bare-prefix stub fails CI per the same rule above)'. Fix-round-4 also added it to §6 024B line 190: 'for blocked-on-adopter-conflict: requires TF-024X-conflict-<adopter> reference with meaningful content'. §5.2 is the per-adopter onboarding contract — it is the PRIMARY reference that a cascade-slice author (024C–024F) reads to understand what a blocked-on-adopter-conflict close requires. A slice author following §5.2 alone would file a bare-prefix TF reference (no status field, no description) and believe they have satisfied the contract. That bare-prefix reference would fail the CI check specified in invariant 2 — but the slice author would not know this from §5.2. This is the same propagation-gap pattern: fix-round-4 added the requirement to the definitive spec site (invariant 2) and the acceptance-criterion site (§6 024B) but not to the contract-view site (§5.2). The gap is MAJ because: (a) §5.2 is the direct reading path for cascade-slice authors; (b) a CI failure at 024C/024D/024E/024F submission time caused by a missing meaningful-content requirement the author never knew about defeats the purpose of specifying the requirement in the plan-doc; (c) the pattern has now recurred four rounds in a row — fix-round-4 was already the propagation-gap fix, but it only propagated to three of four sites.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-completeness-r5.json:146:      \"evidence\": \"Plan-doc line 208 (§7 risk surface, R-024-06 mitigation column, CI enforcement paragraph): 'check-invocation-log-entry.sh (a) verifies the TF-024X-conflict-<adopter> reference IS present in DISPATCH-NOTE AND (b) actively asserts that docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md was NOT modified in the PR diff.' Assertion (a) says only 'reference IS present' — no meaningful-content qualifier. Fix-round-4 added the meaningful-content requirement to invariant 2 line 47 ('with meaningful content (status field + non-empty description; bare-prefix stub fails CI per the same rule above)') and §6 024B line 190 ('for blocked-on-adopter-conflict: requires TF-024X-conflict-<adopter> reference with meaningful content'). R-024-06 is the risk-table row that Codex executors read when implementing check-invocation-log-entry.sh. R4-NEW-MAJ-001 (just closed) established that R-024-06 is a normative implementation-guidance anchor: when R-024-06 omits a requirement that invariant 2 specifies, a Codex executor implementing from R-024-06 alone builds the deficient version. Fix-round-4 fixed the negative-assertion gap in R-024-06 (adding '(b) actively asserts log file was NOT modified') but the same edit introduced a new gap: the TF-reference check (a) is now correctly described for the negative-assertion dimension but still omits the meaningful-content dimension. A Codex executor reading R-024-06 assertion (a) would implement a prefix-presence check only — the same stub-row bypass that R4-NEW-MAJ-002 (safety) closed for the onboarded-operator-bump path. The R-024-06 description of assertion (a) must be brought into conformance with invariant 2 + §6 024B.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/FIX-ROUND-5.md:51:- §6 line 176 (Threshold A telemetry): \"branch-protection-log.md is current (one entry per onboarded adopter)\" → clarified that \"onboarded adopter\" means `cascade-status: onboarded` or `onboarded-operator-bump`; conflict-close slices have NO entry per invariant 2.\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r6.json:13:      \"evidence\": \"§1 item 2 (plan-doc line 30) now reads: '(b) `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` invocation per D-035 with invocation-log entry under `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md` — **conditional on the slice's `cascade-status:` declaration per §5.2 + invariant 2** (only `onboarded` and `onboarded-operator-bump` slices add a log entry; `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` slices MUST NOT modify the log file)'. The conditional carve-out is explicit and cross-references both §5.2 and invariant 2. Consistent with §3 line 84, §5.2 line 133, invariant 2 lines 44-47. R5-NEW-MAJ-001 is CLOSED.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r6.json:55:      \"evidence\": \"**§6 slice plan (plan-doc lines 202-205)** reads:\\n\\n- Line 202: `| 024C | PIM cascade — adopter PR + SCP-side cascade slice + **invocation log** + post-bake observation | ...`\\n- Line 203: `| 024D | control-tower cascade — adopter PR + SCP-side cascade slice + **invocation log** + post-bake observation | ...`\\n- Line 204: `| 024E | mapp-doc-agent + recommender paired cascade — both adopter PRs + SCP-side cascade slice + **2 invocation log entries** + post-bake observations on both | ...`\\n- Line 205: `| 024F | shopify-app cascade — adopter PR + SCP-side cascade slice + **invocation log** + post-bake observation | ...`\\n\\nIn all four rows, the Deliverable column describes 'invocation log' or 'invocation log entries' as a mandatory component of the slice, with no carve-out for the `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` close-state.\\n\\nThis is the identical defect class as R4-NEW-MAJ-002 (§3 unconditional invocation-log claim) and R5-NEW-MAJ-001 (§1 item 2 unconditional invocation-log claim). Both prior rounds established this as MAJ severity for the following reason: a reader forming their model from §6 — the Slice plan, universally consulted by an implementor opening a new cascade slice — would conclude that 024C (for example) MUST ship 'adopter PR + SCP-side cascade slice + invocation log + post-bake observation'. If PIM has a pre-existing policy-check workflow conflict (T-024-10 scenario), the implementor would still include a log entry in the PR. This would trigger the CI negative-assertion check in `check-invocation-log-entry.sh` (invariant 2, `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` path: 'PR MUST NOT modify `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md`') and fail CI.\\n\\nThe inconsistency is directly verifiable:\\n- §1 item 2(b) (fixed at R5): 'conditional on the slice's `cascade-status:` declaration per §5.2 + invariant 2'\\n- §3 line 84 (fixed at R4): 'only when `cascade-status` is `onboarded` or `onboarded-operator-bump`'\\n- §5.2 line 133 (unchanged): 'only when `cascade-status: onboarded` or `onboarded-operator-bump`'\\n- Invariant 2 lines 44-47: explicit three-path model\\n- §6 lines 202-205: 'invocation log' — **no conditional**\\n\\nFive of the relevant loci have the conditional; §6 does not. The pattern of propagation gaps across five rounds (R2 introduction → R3 §3+R-024-06 → R4 §3+§1 missed → R5 §1 caught → R6 §6 slice rows) demonstrates that §6 is the final propagation gap in this clause.\\n\\nNote on 024E line 204: the unconditional '2 invocation log entries' is particularly misleading because it hard-codes a count (2) that assumes both mapp-doc-agent and recommender are on the `onboarded` or `onboarded-operator-bump` path. If one of the two has a conflict, the slice may produce 1 invocation log entry (or 0 if both conflict) rather than 2.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/FIX-ROUND-1.md:26:| **COR-MAJ-001 / MAJ-SAFE-002** | correctness, safety | **INLINE-FIX** | Log path replaced 6× in plan-doc: `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/branch-protection-log.md` → `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md` (the D-035-canonical estate-cascade audit trail; the existing file at that path already reserves itself for WP-SCP-024 cascade entries via its \"Estate cascade rollout = WP-SCP-024\" comment). No script change needed — `enable-required-check.sh` already hardcodes the canonical path; the plan-doc was the drift. |\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/FIX-ROUND-1.md:54:   - 6× path replacement (WP-SCP-024 → WP-SCP-020 branch-protection-log)\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r7.json:7:  \"summary\": \"R6 closure verified at dfae0b8: R6-NEW-MAJ-001 (correctness) — §6 024C/D/E/F Deliverable columns now conditional on cascade-status — CLOSED. Full concept-level sweep per FIX-ROUND-6 pattern observation: every reference to cascade-status enumeration, branch-protection-log.md, invocation log, meaningful-content spec paraphrases, and fail-closed default inspected individually across both plan-doc and DISPATCH-NOTE. Two new defect classes found: (1) Three downstream paraphrase loci for the meaningful-content format spec (§5.2 line 135, §7 R-024-06 line 224, §7 R-024-07 line 225) carry abbreviated descriptions that omit the \\\\S non-whitespace anchor — rated MAJ because an implementor deriving the TF-reference requirement from these loci would write a description that passes the paraphrase but fails the actual \\\\S.{19,} regex check (all-whitespace 20-char bypass unclosed at these loci). (2) DISPATCH-NOTE risk item 4 describes invariant 2's security mitigation as 'three-path CI enforcement', omitting the fail-closed default for absent/unrecognised cascade-status values — rated MIN because both a 'per cascade-status: field' qualifier and 'invariant 2' cross-reference are present in the same sentence, making the fail-closed reachable in one hop; however the fail-closed is the key defence against template-drift bypass and its absence from the risk-surface description is a correctness gap. DISPATCH-NOTE criterion (iii) 'three-path cascade-status' label carries the same gap — also MIN. R7 fixpoint is NOT reached: 0 new CRIT + 1 new MAJ + 2 new MIN.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r7.json:33:      \"title\": \"Complete invocation-log and branch-protection-log.md sweep — no unconditional claims found outside identified loci\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r7.json:34:      \"evidence\": \"All occurrences of 'invocation log', 'branch-protection-log', 'invocation-log' in plan-doc and DISPATCH-NOTE assessed:\\n\\nPLAN-DOC:\\n- Line 10 (D-044 reservation): 'invocation-log convention extension' — topic scope label, not normative claim. Clean.\\n- Line 11 (D-045 reservation): 'branch-protection invocation log, post-bake observation window' — topic scope label. Clean.\\n- Line 30 (§1 item 2): '...with invocation-log entry under `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md` — **conditional on the slice's `cascade-status:` declaration per §5.2 + invariant 2**'. CONDITIONAL PRESENT. Clean.\\n- Line 44 (invariant 2 lead-in): 'with an invocation log entry under `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md` ... An apply without the log entry is unrecorded.' — describes the log-entry content for the invocation case. The `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` path involves no invocation, so 'an apply without the log entry' accurately captures the nominal-invocation scenario. Immediately followed by the cascade-status conditional sub-bullets (lines 45-47). Contextually correct.\\n- Lines 45/46/47 (invariant 2 sub-bullets): 'MUST modify' or 'MUST NOT modify' branch-protection-log.md explicitly per path. CONDITIONAL PRESENT. Clean.\\n- Line 89 (§3): '...with invocation-log entry under `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md` **only when `cascade-status` is `onboarded` or `onboarded-operator-bump`**'. CONDITIONAL PRESENT. Clean.\\n- Lines 98-100 (§4 T-024-01/02/03): T-024-01 describes the log's audit function in an invocation scenario; T-024-02 describes forensic detectability via absence; T-024-03 is explicitly the post-invocation-rejection scenario. Per R6-SCAN-3 analysis, all three are scenario-scoped, not normative per-slice deliverable claims. No propagation gap.\\n- Line 135 (§5.2 blocked-on-adopter-conflict): 'the PR MUST NOT modify `branch-protection-log.md` (CI script asserts absence of modification per invariant 2)' — correct negative assertion. But parenthetical paraphrase of the TF-reference spec omits `\\\\S` — see R7-NEW-MAJ-001.\\n- Line 138 (§5.2 SCP-side cascade slice PR): 'invocation log entry (D-035) — only when `cascade-status: onboarded` or `onboarded-operator-bump`'. CONDITIONAL PRESENT. Clean.\\n- Line 171 (§5.4): 'consumes a captured before-state from a prior invocation log entry' — restore mode consumes a prior log entry. Rollback presupposes a prior invocation (onboarded/onboarded-operator-bump path). Clean.\\n- Line 192 (§5.6 Threshold A): 'one entry per adopter that closed with `cascade-status: onboarded` or `cascade-status: onboarded-operator-bump`; conflict-close slices have NO entry per invariant 2's negative assertion'. CONDITIONAL PRESENT. Clean.\\n- Line 244 (§8 Threshold A): 'Adopters whose cascade slice closed with `cascade-status: blocked-on-adopter-conflict` have NO log entry (per invariant 2's negative assertion)'. CONDITIONAL PRESENT. Clean.\\n\\nDISPATCH-NOTE:\\n- Line 51 (criterion iii): 'invocation-logged + CI-enforced' — functional label, not a log-entry mandate per se. Cross-references 'positive evidence + meaningful-content regex match + negative assertion where applicable per invariant 2'. Clean.\\n- Line 69 (risk item 4): '`onboarded` and `onboarded-operator-bump` paths require log entry + target match; `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` path requires log file NOT modified'. CONDITIONAL PRESENT per the three named paths; fail-closed omission addressed in R7-NEW-MIN-001.\\n- Line 80 (R1 review surface): 'invocation-log / no-CI-write / operator-only constraints' — review-focus descriptor, not a normative claim. Clean.\\n\\nNo unconditional invocation-log or branch-protection-log.md claims found outside the loci already addressed in findings R7-NEW-MAJ-001 and R7-NEW-MIN-001.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness.json:5:  \"summary\": \"One MAJ finding: the plan-doc consistently routes invocation-log entries to docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/branch-protection-log.md, directly contradicting D-035 (which canonises docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md) and the existing log file at that path, which explicitly reserves space for WP-SCP-024 cascade entries. All other references (D-031, D-035, D-036, D-040, D-028, TF-023E-001/002, sequencing, invariant/slice-plan consistency, FLA-independence invariant 4) are accurate. Two minor/nit findings below the MAJ threshold are also recorded.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness.json:11:      \"evidence\": \"D-035 (DECISIONS.md line 82) specifies the log path as 'docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md': '...emits an invocation-log block ... for paste into docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md. The log commit is part of the invocation procedure: an apply without the log entry is considered unrecorded.' The existing file at that path (docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md line 35) reads '_(no invocations recorded yet ... Estate cascade rollout = WP-SCP-024.)_' — explicitly designed to receive WP-SCP-024 cascade entries. The plan-doc, however, consistently specifies a different path at six locations: §1 item 2 ('invocation-log entry under docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/branch-protection-log.md'), invariant 2 ('invocation log entry under docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/branch-protection-log.md'), §3 implementation-slices bullet ('(b) the invocation-log entry under docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/branch-protection-log.md'), §5.2 item 2 ('docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/branch-protection-log.md invocation log entry (D-035)'), §5.5 Threshold A bullet 2 ('docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/branch-protection-log.md is current'), and §8 Threshold A bullet 2 ('docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/branch-protection-log.md'). Implementation slices dispatched against this plan-doc will create or populate a new WP-SCP-024-path file, leaving the D-035-canonical WP-SCP-020 log permanently empty despite its explicit provision for cascade entries — creating a split audit trail and contradicting the ratified doctrine.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness.json:12:      \"recommendation\": \"Choose one of two resolutions and apply it consistently: (a) Update all six plan-doc occurrences of 'docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/branch-protection-log.md' to 'docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md', aligning with the D-035 canonical path and the existing log file's stated purpose. (b) If a WP-SCP-024-specific log is the intended architecture (e.g. to keep per-WP audit trails separate), amend D-035 with a forward-note that the estate cascade log lives at the WP-SCP-024 path, update the existing WP-SCP-020 log file header to redirect to the new path, and ensure the plan-doc body explicitly justifies the divergence from D-035 rather than silently citing D-035 as the authority for the log convention. Do NOT leave six plan-doc references pointing at a path that D-035 and the existing file contradict.\"\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r5.json:13:      \"evidence\": \"R-024-06 mitigation (plan-doc line 208) now reads: 'CI enforcement (invariant 2): when `cascade-status: blocked-on-adopter-conflict`, `check-invocation-log-entry.sh` (a) verifies the `TF-024X-conflict-<adopter>` reference IS present in DISPATCH-NOTE AND (b) actively asserts that `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md` was NOT modified in the PR diff (closes the R3 safety bypass — a slice declaring conflict-close while actually invoking the script must fail CI). Both assertions are required; one positive + one negative.' This replaces the old 'skips log-entry assertion ... verifies the TF reference is present' language. Both the positive TF-reference assertion and the negative log-NOT-modified assertion are explicitly named. The 'carve-out' framing has been retired in this context. Fully consistent with invariant 2's three-path check-path model and §6 024B acceptance row. R4-NEW-MAJ-001 is CLOSED.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r5.json:20:      \"evidence\": \"§3 (plan-doc line 73) now reads: '(b) the invocation-log entry under `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md` **only when `cascade-status` is `onboarded` or `onboarded-operator-bump`** (when `cascade-status: blocked-on-adopter-conflict`, no log entry is added — the CI asserts log-file was NOT modified per invariant 2)'. The closing parenthetical '(c) any SCP-side ADOPT-001 / scaffolder amendments' follows. The sentence is now conditional and explicitly cross-references invariant 2. The closure tag 'Closes 024A R4 R4-NEW-MAJ-002 (correctness — §3 unconditional invocation-log claim contradicted invariant 2's conditional carve-out)' is appended to the §3 paragraph. Consistent with §5.2 line 122 and invariant 2 lines 44–47. R4-NEW-MAJ-002 (correctness) is CLOSED at §3.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r5.json:27:      \"evidence\": \"**§1 Purpose item 2 (plan-doc line 30)** reads:\\n\\n'2. Defines a **per-adopter onboarding contract** that each cascade slice satisfies: (a) caller wrapper PR'd into the adopter repo with SCP federation primitive SHA-pinned per VERSIONING.md (D-036); **(b) `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` invocation per D-035 with invocation-log entry under `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md`**; (c) post-bake observation window of ≥1 calendar week before cascade declares the adopter onboarded; (d) opt-in scorecard-emit ...'\\n\\nItem (b) is stated unconditionally. The description 'that each cascade slice satisfies' makes this apply to all cascade slices with no carve-out.\\n\\nThis is the identical defect class as R4-NEW-MAJ-002, which was found and fixed at §3. The fix-round-4 edit correctly applied the conditional carve-out to §3 and §5.2 line 122, but did not propagate to §1 — the highest-level reader entry-point for the plan-doc. A first-time reader of §1 (the Purpose summary, universally read first) forms the model that ALL cascade slices ship (a) wrapper + (b) invocation-log entry + (c) post-bake window. When implementing a `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` cascade slice, such a reader would include a log entry in the PR. This would trigger the CI negative-assertion failure in `check-invocation-log-entry.sh` that invariant 2 is specifically designed to catch (invariant 2 line 47: 'PR MUST NOT modify `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md`' for that close-state).\\n\\nThe inconsistency is directly verifiable: §1 item 2(b) says 'invocation-log entry' unconditionally; §3 line 73 now says 'only when `cascade-status` is `onboarded` or `onboarded-operator-bump`'; §5.2 line 122 says 'only when `cascade-status: onboarded` or `onboarded-operator-bump`'; invariant 2 line 47 says 'PR MUST NOT modify ... (no `enable-required-check.sh` invocation occurred)'. Three of the four loci were updated by fix-round-3 / fix-round-4; §1 Purpose item 2 was not.\\n\\nThis is the same propagation gap pattern identified in FIX-ROUND-4.md ('when adding a carve-out clause, enumerate the full set of states ... fix-round-3's edit added the negative assertion for one path but didn't propagate it through R-024-06 or §3'). This round's fix correctly propagated to §3 but missed §1.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r5.json:28:      \"recommendation\": \"Update §1 Purpose item 2(b) to add the same conditional carve-out applied to §3 at fix-round-4. Replace:\\n\\n'(b) `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` invocation per D-035 with invocation-log entry under `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md`'\\n\\nwith:\\n\\n'(b) `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` invocation per D-035 with invocation-log entry under `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md` — **only when `cascade-status: onboarded` or `onboarded-operator-bump`**; conflict-close (`blocked-on-adopter-conflict`) slices ship no log entry (CI asserts log NOT modified per invariant 2)'\\n\\nThis makes §1 consistent with §3, §5.2 line 122, and invariant 2 — all four loci in the model that determine how a cascade-slice executor understands the log-entry requirement.\"\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r4.json:6:  \"summary\": \"R3-NEW-MAJ-001 (safety) closure is REAL: invariant 2's blocked-on-adopter-conflict bullet now explicitly asserts 'PR MUST NOT modify branch-protection-log.md', §5.2 item (2) echoes 'CI script asserts absence of modification per invariant 2', and §6 024B acceptance row requires 'asserts log file was NOT modified'. All three sites are consistent. The bypass surface R3 identified is closed. However, the surface scan finds two new issues: (1) R-024-06 in the risk table was NOT updated by fix-round-3 — it still says only 'verifies the TF reference is present', omitting the new log-file-absence assertion; this is an internal inconsistency that a Codex executor implementing from the risk table alone would implement only half the check (MAJ). (2) The onboarded-operator-bump CI check verifies only that STATUS.md contains the standardised prefix string — it does not require the row to carry any meaningful content (e.g. a Renovate PR URL, an open/in-progress marker, or any field beyond the bare slug). A stub row (just the prefix + adopter slug, no status) passes CI; the Threshold A credit mechanism is then entirely reviewer-dependent with no machine-verifiable signal that a real Renovate cycle occurred. This is the same negative-assertion gap pattern as R3-NEW-MAJ-001 safety: positive evidence is verified (prefix present), but no minimum-content requirement is asserted (MAJ). R4 fixpoint criterion: 0 new CRIT + 0 new MAJ — not met.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r4.json:12:      \"evidence\": \"Three plan-doc sites verified as consistent: (a) Invariant 2, blocked-on-adopter-conflict bullet: 'PR MUST NOT modify docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md (no enable-required-check.sh invocation occurred — closing the bypass surface that R3 safety surfaced) AND MUST reference the filed TF-024X-conflict-<adopter> TF in DISPATCH-NOTE matching the standardised prefix.' (b) §5.2 item (2), blocked-on-adopter-conflict value description: 'the PR MUST NOT modify branch-protection-log.md (CI script asserts absence of modification per invariant 2).' (c) §6 024B acceptance row: 'for blocked-on-adopter-conflict: requires TF-024X-conflict-<adopter> reference AND asserts log file was NOT modified (closes the R3 safety bypass).' All three sites specify both the positive assertion (TF reference present) and the negative assertion (log file NOT modified). FIX-ROUND-3.md claim matches. The bypass path described in R3-NEW-MAJ-001 — declare blocked-on-adopter-conflict, invoke enable-required-check.sh, commit the log entry, file the TF reference, pass CI — is closed because the CI script now also asserts log-file unmodified.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r4.json:20:      \"recommendation\": \"Amend R-024-06 mitigation to: 'T-024-10 mitigation: cascade slice CLOSES with status blocked-on-adopter-conflict per invariant 10 (forward TF TF-024X-conflict-<adopter> owned by the slice author; new sub-slice opens on conflict resolution). The CI invocation-log check (invariant 2 carve-out) for these close-state slices: (a) MUST NOT find branch-protection-log.md modified in the PR diff (assert log file unmodified — no enable-required-check.sh invocation occurred); (b) MUST find a TF-024X-conflict-<adopter> reference in DISPATCH-NOTE matching the standardised prefix. Both assertions are required; either failure causes CI to fail.' This makes R-024-06 consistent with invariant 2, §5.2, and §6 024B.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r4.json:42:      \"evidence\": \"Invariant 2, onboarded-operator-bump bullet: 'PR MUST modify docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md (target match as above) AND STATUS.md MUST contain a row matching the standardised prefix TF-024X-renovate-<adopter-slug>.' The log-file modification requirement is present and carries the same target-match check as the onboarded path. A cascade slice cannot declare onboarded-operator-bump and skip the log entry — the CI check requires the log file to be modified. This path does not introduce a new log-file bypass. The bypass surface on this path is limited to the TF row content issue (R4-NEW-MAJ-002).\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r9.json:26:      \"evidence\": \"Systematic scan of plan-doc bypass surface post fix-round-8: (1) Invariant 2 fail-closed default (line 44): 'if cascade-status: is absent OR contains a value not in the enumerated set {onboarded, onboarded-operator-bump, blocked-on-adopter-conflict}, the script exits non-zero (CI fails)' — PRESENT, UNCHANGED. (2) Invariant 2 regex (line 52): 'TF-024X-(renovate|conflict)-[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)+(?:\\\\*\\\\*)? \\\\((open|pending|in-progress|closed)\\\\): \\\\S.{19,}' — PRESENT, IDENTICAL to R8-verified pattern. (3) Invariant 2 six worked examples (lines 58-63): all six present, unchanged from R8. (4) Conditionality of invocation-log entry (lines 44-47, 138): log entry required ONLY for onboarded and onboarded-operator-bump; conflict-close path explicitly MUST NOT modify log file — UNCHANGED. (5) §3 programme protocol negative assertion (line 89): 'when cascade-status: blocked-on-adopter-conflict, no log entry is added — the CI asserts log-file was NOT modified per invariant 2' — PRESENT, UNCHANGED. (6) §5.2 conflict-close path (line 135): 'PR MUST NOT modify branch-protection-log.md' — PRESENT, UNCHANGED. (7) R-024-06 negative assertion (line 224): 'actively asserts that docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md was NOT modified in the PR diff' — PRESENT, UNCHANGED. (8) §8 Threshold A blocked-on-adopter-conflict exclusion (lines 243-244): conflict-close adopters excluded from '≥3 of 5' count — PRESENT, UNCHANGED. (9) §5.6 Threshold A criteria Renovate-only gate (line 191): 'via Renovate (proving version-skew tolerance)' present — UNCHANGED (R8-NEW-MIN-001 carry-forward deferral still applies; not a new MIN at R9). No unconditional log-entry sites. No stale 'three-path' language. No pre-R6 'non-empty description' paraphrase. No new bypass path introduced by fix-round-8.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r4-package.json:4:  \"instruction\": \"SAFETY/BYPASS R4 review of WP-SCP-024 slice 024A plan-doc at branch feature/wp-scp-024-estate-cascade-plan, HEAD commit d13157f (fix-round-3 applied). Read FIX-ROUND-3.md. Verify R3 closure: R3-NEW-MAJ-001 (safety) — invariant 2 now requires CI to assert `branch-protection-log.md` was NOT modified when `cascade-status: blocked-on-adopter-conflict` declared. §6 024B acceptance row enumerates the no-modification assertion. Verify NO new safety/bypass surface from fix-round-3 edits — particularly: does `cascade-status: onboarded-operator-bump` open any new bypass (e.g., a slice declares onboarded-operator-bump but never actually files the TF-024X-renovate row)? The CI script as specified verifies STATUS.md contains the row, but is the row's content meaningful (i.e., does the row itself need to reference an open TF-NNN identifier, or just contain the prefix string)? NEVER recommend descoping. Output to docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r4.json.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r6.json:12:      \"evidence\": \"§1 item 2 (plan-doc line 30): '(b) scripts/enable-required-check.sh invocation per D-035 with invocation-log entry under docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md — conditional on the slice's cascade-status: declaration per §5.2 + invariant 2 (only onboarded and onboarded-operator-bump slices add a log entry; blocked-on-adopter-conflict slices MUST NOT modify the log file)'. The unconditional 'each cascade slice satisfies' defect (R5's §1 propagation gap) is resolved. The explicit MUST NOT is present. Consistent with §3, §5.2, invariant 2, R-024-06, §8. No residual bypass from §1.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r6.json:64:      \"evidence\": \"Full scan of the plan-doc for carve-out patterns: (1) Invariant 2 onboarded path ('PR MUST modify branch-protection-log.md AND the modified entry's target repo string MUST match') — two positive assertions, no negative. Already reviewed at R5 (R5-SCAN-ONBOARDED-PATH-NEGATIVE-ASSERTION) — no bypass here because the bypass incentive runs the other direction (an attacker would want to NOT modify the log, not modify it extra). The onboarded path's positive-only design is load-bearing. (2) Invariant 3 ('cascade does NOT touch existing adopter content') — this is a scope-limiter, not a CI-enforced assertion. No CI enforcement is claimed for it; it is policy. A cascade slice that violates invariant 3 would be caught at review (the adopter-side PR would show modifications to files other than the wrapper). Not a CI bypass surface. (3) §5.2 item 1 (adopter PR shape) — describes PR body contents (cost estimate, bus-factor disclosure, etc.) as requirements but there is no CI enforcement of PR body contents. This is an acceptance-reviewer dependency, not a bypass surface — the 3-lens R1 review covers the SCP-side cascade slice PR, not the adopter-side PR contents. No new CI bypass surface. (4) Threshold A criterion in §8: '≥3 of 5 cohort adopters have required-check live + ≥1 Renovate cycle propagated cleanly' — USER-GATE-E is a human gate signed by @jrnb2024. Threshold A is not CI-enforced; it is operator-signed. This is by design. Reviewer-dependence at Threshold A is appropriate for a gate that requires human judgment (checking Renovate PR URLs, observing clean CI runs). Not a CI bypass surface. (5) §5.2 item 2 blocked-on-adopter-conflict description — references 'meaningful content per invariant 2's regex spec' which is the correct cross-reference. The cross-reference is present. Consistent with invariant 2. (6) R-024-07 'with meaningful content (status field + non-empty description after the slug)' — still uses 'non-empty description' language (same propagation gap as §6, but R-024-07 also says 'per invariant 2' as a cross-reference which partially mitigates). The 'per invariant 2' cross-reference is present in R-024-07 so the regex spec applies. However, the local 'non-empty description' language contradicts the regex's ≥20-char floor — see R6-NEW-MAJ-001 (the regex itself has the whitespace inconsistency). No additional standalone bypass from R-024-07's 'non-empty description' because it explicitly defers to invariant 2. After R6-NEW-MAJ-001 is fixed at invariant 2, R-024-07 inherits the fix via the cross-reference. No separate fix needed at R-024-07 on this specific point.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r6.json:71:      \"evidence\": \"R-024-06 mitigation (plan-doc line 219): 'CI enforcement (invariant 2): when cascade-status: blocked-on-adopter-conflict, check-invocation-log-entry.sh (a) verifies the TF-024X-conflict-<adopter> reference IS present in DISPATCH-NOTE with meaningful content matching invariant 2's regex spec (status field + ≥20 char description; bare-prefix stubs fail CI) AND (b) actively asserts that docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md was NOT modified in the PR diff. All three checks are required; positive evidence + meaningful-content format match + negative assertion.' The phrase 'with meaningful content matching invariant 2's regex spec' is the correct reference. This site correctly defers to invariant 2 for the format spec. Once R6-NEW-MAJ-001 is fixed at invariant 2 (aligning regex and prose), R-024-06 inherits the fix via the cross-reference. No standalone fix needed at R-024-06 beyond ensuring the invariant 2 fix propagates.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/FIX-ROUND-4.md:27:| **R4-NEW-MAJ-002** (correctness) | correctness | **INLINE-FIX** | §3 line 73 (programme-protocol section) said implementation slices ship \"(b) the invocation-log entry under `branch-protection-log.md`\" UNCONDITIONALLY. Post fix-round-3, the conflict-close path explicitly forbids modifying that file. **Fix:** §3 line 73 reworded to make item (b) conditional on `cascade-status` being `onboarded` or `onboarded-operator-bump`; conflict-close path explicitly noted as no-log-entry. |\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r3.json:33:      \"evidence\": \"Invariant 2's conflict-close CI check is specified as: 'the CI script verifies the TF reference exists and matches the standardised prefix.' No additional check is specified. The plan-doc's rationale for the carve-out is: 'no enable-required-check.sh was invoked because the conflict was discovered before invocation' — this is a narrative assertion, not a machine-verified property. The CI script (check-invocation-log-entry.sh, landing at 024B) is spec'd to parse cascade-status: and apply the appropriate check; for blocked-on-adopter-conflict the 'appropriate check' is TF-reference presence only. The spec does not require the script to also assert: (a) docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md was NOT modified in this PR diff, or (b) no new entry matching the PR's target adopter appears in the log file. Consequence: a cascade-slice PR can include both a new log entry (from enable-required-check.sh invocation) AND a TF reference (to satisfy the conflict-close check), declare cascade-status: blocked-on-adopter-conflict, and merge without the log-entry CI check ever firing. The API mutation is invisible to the audit trail: the log entry is committed under the conflict-close exemption path, and the onboarded CI path (which would verify the log entry's target matches the DISPATCH-NOTE target) is bypassed by the cascade-status declaration. This is the specific bypass surface the R3 package asked about: 'does the cascade-status field create a path for a malicious slice to declare blocked-on-adopter-conflict to skip the log-entry check while actually invoking enable-required-check.sh?' The answer from the current plan-doc spec is: yes, because the conflict-close CI check has no invocation-footprint absence check.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r3.json:34:      \"recommendation\": \"Amend the invariant 2 conflict-close CI spec to require that check-invocation-log-entry.sh ALSO asserts, for cascade-status: blocked-on-adopter-conflict PRs: docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md was NOT modified in this PR diff (i.e., git diff shows zero additions to that file). If the log file WAS modified in a conflict-close PR, the CI script must FAIL with an error message indicating that log-file modification is incompatible with blocked-on-adopter-conflict status. Amend §6 024B acceptance row to require: 'check-invocation-log-entry.sh rejects conflict-close PRs where branch-protection-log.md was modified (log-file-presence-in-diff is incompatible with blocked-on-adopter-conflict status)' as a fourth enumerated check. This closes the invocation-footprint bypass path. The fix does not require checking API call traces — git PR diff is sufficient because enable-required-check.sh emits its log block for the operator to paste in; if the operator invoked the script, the log entry appears in the PR diff.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r3.json:55:      \"evidence\": \"The plan-doc assigns one named cascade slice per adopter (024C=PIM, 024D=CT, 024E=mapp-doc-agent+recommender, 024F=shopify-app). A conflict-close path produces a sub-slice; the original slice closes with blocked-on-adopter-conflict and the sub-slice closes with onboarded. Two onboarded closures for the same adopter would require two distinct PR sequences, each with a distinct log entry in branch-protection-log.md. check-invocation-log-entry.sh verifies that the PR modifies the log file with a matching entry — it does not check for pre-existing entries for the same adopter. Two log entries for the same adopter + branch would be visible to the 024G reviewer. The enable-required-check.sh script is idempotent (re-running against an already-protected repo applies the same settings; no destructive mutation). Duplicate onboarded declarations are an audit-integrity concern (two entries, one PR referencing each) but not a security bypass — the branch protection state is idempotent, the log file accumulates both entries, and the 024G reviewer counts adopters by log-entry presence. An attacker who files a duplicate onboarded slice gains nothing: the branch protection is already enabled, and both log entries are visible. Assessed as NIT.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r3.json:59:  \"r3_verdict_rationale\": \"R3 found 1 new MAJ (R3-NEW-MAJ-001: conflict-close CI carve-out has no invocation-footprint absence check, enabling a bypass where a malicious cascade slice invokes enable-required-check.sh and escapes the audit trail by declaring blocked-on-adopter-conflict). This directly answers the package's primary safety question affirmatively: the cascade-status field DOES create a path for a malicious slice to invoke the script while skipping the log-entry check, because the conflict-close CI check verifies only TF-reference presence, not log-file unmodified. The R3 fixpoint criterion (0 new CRIT + 0 new MAJ) is not met. Fix required: amend the invariant 2 conflict-close CI spec to require check-invocation-log-entry.sh to assert that branch-protection-log.md was NOT modified in a blocked-on-adopter-conflict PR diff. Once that fix lands, R3 must run a verification cycle.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/FIX-ROUND-3.md:27:| **R3-NEW-MAJ-001 (safety)** | safety | **INLINE-FIX** | The conflict-close CI check verified TF-reference presence but did NOT assert that `branch-protection-log.md` was NOT modified. A malicious slice could invoke `enable-required-check.sh`, include the log entry, declare `cascade-status: blocked-on-adopter-conflict`, file the TF, and bypass the audit trail. **Fix:** invariant 2 now says when `cascade-status: blocked-on-adopter-conflict` is declared, CI MUST assert the log file was NOT modified in the PR diff. §6 024B acceptance row explicitly enumerates this assertion. |\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r4.json:13:      \"evidence\": \"Four loci verified against plan-doc at d13157f:\\n\\n(1) **Invariant 2 (plan-doc line 44–47):** The CI enforcement paragraph now names three values in the enum header — `onboarded`, `onboarded-operator-bump`, `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` — and enumerates a distinct bulleted check-path for each. The `onboarded-operator-bump` bullet (line 46) reads: 'PR MUST modify `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md` (target match as above) AND STATUS.md MUST contain a row matching the standardised prefix `TF-024X-renovate-<adopter-slug>`.' This is a well-specified third path distinct from both the Renovate path (log entry required, no TF row required) and the conflict path (log NOT modified, TF-conflict reference required).\\n\\n(2) **§5.2 item (2) (plan-doc lines 116–121):** The DISPATCH-NOTE field spec now explicitly says '**three** values' and defines all three with prose descriptions. `onboarded-operator-bump` is defined at line 118: 'R-024-07 fallback path; an `enable-required-check.sh` invocation occurred + the log entry is in this PR + bake satisfied via operator-driven SHA pin bump (NOT Renovate); STATUS.md gains a `TF-024X-renovate-<adopter-slug>` row gating Threshold A credit (per R-024-07).' Internally consistent with invariant 2.\\n\\n(3) **§6 024B acceptance row (plan-doc line 190):** The acceptance criterion for `check-invocation-log-entry.sh` now enumerates all three close-states: 'for `onboarded`: requires log entry + target match; for `onboarded-operator-bump`: requires log entry + target match + `TF-024X-renovate-<adopter-slug>` STATUS.md row; for `blocked-on-adopter-conflict`: requires `TF-024X-conflict-<adopter>` reference AND asserts log file was NOT modified.' All three paths present.\\n\\n(4) **R-024-07 (plan-doc line 209):** Declares `cascade-status: onboarded-operator-bump` as the DISPATCH-NOTE signal for the Renovate-miss path, specifies the TF-row standardised prefix, and closes '024A R3 R3-NEW-MAJ-001 (correctness — third cascade-status value enumerated).' Internally consistent with invariant 2 and §5.2.\\n\\nAll four loci required by R3's recommendation are present and internally consistent. R3-NEW-MAJ-001 (correctness) is CLOSED.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r4.json:20:      \"evidence\": \"**R-024-06 mitigation (plan-doc line 208)** reads: 'The CI invocation-log check (invariant 2 carve-out) **skips log-entry assertion** for these close-state slices but verifies the TF reference is present.'\\n\\nThis description was accurate under the two-value model that existed before fix-round-3: the CI script had a 'carve-out' that simply skipped the log-entry check for `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` slices and verified the TF reference instead. Fix-round-3 changed the semantics in invariant 2 and §5.2: the CI script now does NOT 'skip' the log-entry check — it actively **asserts the log file was NOT modified** (invariant 2 line 47: 'PR MUST NOT modify `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md`'; §6 024B line 190: 'asserts log file was NOT modified'). These are materially different behaviours:\\n\\n- 'Skip log-entry assertion' = CI is silent about whether the log was modified; only TF reference verified.\\n- 'Assert log file was NOT modified' = CI actively rejects the PR if the log WAS modified; TF reference also required.\\n\\nR-024-06's mitigation still says 'skips log-entry assertion' — which describes the OLD behaviour that R3-NEW-MAJ-001 (safety) identified as a bypass surface. The fix-round-3 edit closed that bypass by converting the skip into a negative assertion, but did not update R-024-06. As a result, R-024-06 now describes a CI behaviour that is weaker than what invariant 2 specifies. A reader implementing the CI script from R-024-06 alone would build the weaker (bypassable) version. The fix-round-3 edit created an internal inconsistency between the definitive spec (invariant 2 + §6 024B) and the risk-table row (R-024-06) for exactly this close-state.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r4.json:21:      \"recommendation\": \"Update R-024-06 mitigation sentence to replace 'The CI invocation-log check (invariant 2 carve-out) skips log-entry assertion for these close-state slices but verifies the TF reference is present' with language consistent with invariant 2: 'The CI `check-invocation-log-entry.sh` script applies the `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` check-path per invariant 2: it asserts the `branch-protection-log.md` was NOT modified in the PR diff (no invocation occurred) AND verifies the `TF-024X-conflict-<adopter>` TF reference is present in DISPATCH-NOTE.' The phrase 'carve-out' should be retired in this context — it was accurate for the old skip-model but is misleading for the new negative-assertion model.\"\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r4.json:27:      \"evidence\": \"**§3 (plan-doc line 73)** describes the SCP-side cascade slice PR as shipping '(a) the cascade slice's DISPATCH-NOTE, **(b) the invocation-log entry under `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md`**, (c) any SCP-side ADOPT-001 / scaffolder amendments.' This is stated unconditionally — the sentence applies to 'each adopter cascade slice' with no carve-out for the `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` close-state.\\n\\nHowever, after fix-round-3, invariant 2 (line 47) specifies that for `cascade-status: blocked-on-adopter-conflict` slices, 'PR MUST NOT modify `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md`' — i.e. no invocation-log entry is shipped; the CI script now **actively enforces** absence of modification. §5.2 item (2) (line 122) also correctly carves this out: 'invocation log entry (D-035) — **only when `cascade-status: onboarded` or `onboarded-operator-bump`**.' \\n\\nSo §3, §5.2, and invariant 2 are mutually inconsistent: §3 says every cascade-slice ships item (b); §5.2 line 122 says item (b) is conditional; invariant 2 says item (b) is forbidden for one close-state. A first-time reader of §3 (the protocol-position section, typically read first) will form an incorrect model of what conflict-close slices ship, and may file an invocation-log entry when none occurred — triggering the CI negative-assertion failure that invariant 2 is specifically designed to catch.\\n\\nThis inconsistency existed in the pre-fix-round-3 text to a lesser degree (the old two-value model didn't have a negative assertion), but the fix-round-3 promotion of the log-NOT-modified requirement to a hard CI assertion makes the §3 unconditional claim actively misleading at implementation time. The fix-round-3 edit elevated the severity of this pre-existing gap to MAJ.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r4.json:28:      \"recommendation\": \"Update §3 line 73 to condition item (b) on the cascade-status close-state. Suggested replacement for '(b) the invocation-log entry under `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md`': '(b) the invocation-log entry under `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md` **when `cascade-status: onboarded` or `onboarded-operator-bump`** — NOT included for `cascade-status: blocked-on-adopter-conflict` close-state slices (per invariant 2; CI asserts absence of modification for that close-state)'. This makes §3 consistent with §5.2 line 122 and invariant 2 line 47.\"\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r2.json:10:      \"title\": \"COR-MAJ-001 closure: all 6 branch-protection-log path occurrences corrected to WP-SCP-020\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r2.json:11:      \"evidence\": \"grep confirms zero occurrences of 'WP-SCP-024/branch-protection-log' in docs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md. All 6 plan-doc locations (§1 item 2, invariant 2, §3, §5.2 item 2, §5.6 Threshold A bullet 2, §8 Threshold A bullet 2) now read 'docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md'. Invariant 2 also carries the explanatory parenthetical '(the D-035-canonical estate-cascade audit trail; the file's existing comment \\\"Estate cascade rollout = WP-SCP-024\\\" reserves it for this WP)' confirming the correct canonical path.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety.json:11:      \"evidence\": \"docs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md §2 invariant 2: 'An apply without the log entry is unrecorded. Cascade slices that fail to commit the invocation log are blocked from declaring the adopter onboarded.' The blocking mechanism is unstated. §5.2 says the SCP-side cascade slice PR ships the invocation-log entry — but this is the same PR that the orchestrator merges; nothing in the CI pipeline checks that `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/branch-protection-log.md` was modified in the PR, or that the entry's target repo matches the slice's DISPATCH-NOTE target. The plan-doc also defers ratification of invariant 2 'in code form' to 024B via D-044 (§9 D-044 row: 'Ratifies invariants 2 + 3 + 10 in code form') — meaning at 024A merge there is no code-enforced check.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety.json:12:      \"recommendation\": \"Add a CI step (e.g. a shell script checked into `scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh`) that asserts: (a) `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/branch-protection-log.md` was modified in the PR diff, and (b) the modified entry contains the target repo string extracted from the DISPATCH-NOTE. Gate 024B's D-044 on this CI step existing — do not allow 024C (first cascade slice against a real adopter repo) to open until the enforcement mechanism is committed. The plan-doc must name this CI check as a hard predecessor for 024C, not just a D-044 deliverable aspiration.\"\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety.json:18:      \"evidence\": \"scripts/enable-required-check.sh line 436: `LOG_FILE=\\\"docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md\\\"`. The emitted log block (lines 443–476) instructs the operator: 'Append the block below to ${LOG_FILE} on the SCP repo'. The plan-doc requires entries in `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/branch-protection-log.md` (§5.2 point 2, §3, and §8 Threshold A criteria row 2: 'Each onboarded adopter has a corresponding entry in `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/branch-protection-log.md`'). A cascade operator following the script's emitted instructions will paste the entry into the WP-SCP-020 log, leaving the WP-SCP-024 log absent. The Threshold A criterion requiring per-adopter entries in the WP-SCP-024 log will then appear unsatisfied even after successful onboarding.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety.json:19:      \"recommendation\": \"Before 024C opens, add a `--log-file` argument to `enable-required-check.sh` (defaulting to the existing WP-SCP-020 path for backward compatibility) so cascade invocations can pass `--log-file docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/branch-protection-log.md` explicitly. Document the required argument in the §5.4 extension spec and in the D-044 ratification row. Alternatively, teach the script to accept a `SCP_LOG_FILE` env var and set it in each cascade slice's DISPATCH-NOTE invocation block. The fix must land in 024B before any cascade slice runs the script against a real adopter repo.\"\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r2-package.json:4:  \"instruction\": \"CORRECTNESS R2 review of WP-SCP-024 slice 024A plan-doc at branch feature/wp-scp-024-estate-cascade-plan, HEAD commit 79b2c1b (fix-round-1 applied). Read FIX-ROUND-1.md. Verify (1) COR-MAJ-001 closure: all 6 occurrences in the plan-doc now reference docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md (NOT WP-SCP-024); (2) COR-MIN-001 closure: DISPATCH-NOTE criterion (iii) now enumerates 10 invariants including 'Cascade does NOT touch existing adopter content'; (3) COR-nit-001 closure: invariant 7 uses `<pre-state.json>` notation matching §5.4. ALSO verify NO new CRIT/MAJ introduced by the fix edits — particularly the new §5.5 (Cross-repo coordination) is internally consistent with §5.6 + §6 + §10. NEVER recommend descoping. Output to docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r2.json with the same shape as review-correctness.json.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r2-package.json:11:    \"docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md\"\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/FIX-ROUND-6.md:85:**6 consecutive rounds caught propagation gaps + new defect classes in this clause.** The clause has now stabilised structurally — every substantive defect class has an explicit spec, and the propagation is now machine-checkable via grep (\"look for every reference to `cascade-status` and `branch-protection-log` and `TF-024X` and ensure each has the conditional + meaningful-content treatment\").\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r5.json:6:  \"summary\": \"R4 closure verification: R4-NEW-MAJ-001 (R-024-06 both-assertion fix) is CLOSED — the risk table mitigation now explicitly names both (a) positive: TF reference IS present AND (b) negative: log file NOT modified; both assertions required. R4-NEW-MAJ-002 (safety — meaningful-content requirement) is CLOSED at the four targeted sites: invariant 2 onboarded-operator-bump bullet, invariant 2 blocked-on-adopter-conflict bullet, §6 024B acceptance row, and R-024-07 mitigation — all now require status field + non-empty description; bare-prefix stubs explicitly fail CI. However, the surface scan finds two new issues: (1) §1 item 2 still describes the per-adopter onboarding contract as unconditionally requiring 'scripts/enable-required-check.sh invocation per D-035 with invocation-log entry under branch-protection-log.md' — the blocked-on-adopter-conflict exception is not acknowledged in §1. Fix-round-4 applied the conditional-log-entry fix to §3 line 73 but NOT to §1 item 2. §1 is presented as a 'each cascade slice satisfies' checklist; a cascade-slice author or Codex executor reading §1 item 2 as their contract summary would believe ALL slices require the log entry. This is the same propagation-gap class that produced R4-NEW-MAJ-002 (correctness) in §3 (MAJ). (2) The 'meaningful content' requirement is specified as 'status field (e.g. (open) / (pending)) AND non-empty description after the prefix slug' — but no column position, no valid-value enumeration, no minimum content length, and no regex or format spec are provided. A Codex executor implementing check-invocation-log-entry.sh cannot implement the meaningful-content check unambiguously from this spec alone: two valid implementations could disagree on whether a single-character description field or a non-enumerated status value passes. The 024B acceptance criterion tests that 'bare-prefix stubs fail CI' but the spec does not define the boundary between a stub and a legitimate row. D-044 defers the scaffolder operational contract but does not explicitly scope the CI script's check-logic format spec — the plan-doc is supposed to be the normative spec that 024B implements against. If the format spec is reviewer-invented at 024B, the protection against the stub-row bypass (R4-NEW-MAJ-002) is no more machine-enforceable than the pre-fix state (MAJ). The R5 fixpoint criterion (0 new CRIT + 0 new MAJ on a complete cycle) is not met.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r5.json:12:      \"evidence\": \"§7 R-024-06 mitigation (plan-doc line 208): 'CI enforcement (invariant 2): when cascade-status: blocked-on-adopter-conflict, check-invocation-log-entry.sh (a) verifies the TF-024X-conflict-<adopter> reference IS present in DISPATCH-NOTE AND (b) actively asserts that docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md was NOT modified in the PR diff (closes the R3 safety bypass — a slice declaring conflict-close while actually invoking the script must fail CI). Both assertions are required; one positive + one negative.' The pre-fix language ('skips log-entry assertion ... verifies the TF reference is present') is gone. The mitigation now names both assertions explicitly and states both are required. Consistent with invariant 2, §5.2, and §6 024B. FIX-ROUND-4.md claim matches. The bypass path — declare blocked-on-adopter-conflict, invoke enable-required-check.sh, commit the log entry, reference the TF, pass CI — is closed because R-024-06 now instructs the CI script to assert log-file unmodified. All four sites (invariant 2, §5.2, §6 024B, R-024-06) are consistent.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r5.json:26:      \"evidence\": \"§1 item 2 (plan-doc line 30): 'Defines a per-adopter onboarding contract that each cascade slice satisfies: (a) caller wrapper PR'd into the adopter repo with SCP federation primitive SHA-pinned per VERSIONING.md (D-036); (b) scripts/enable-required-check.sh invocation per D-035 with invocation-log entry under docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md; (c) post-bake observation window of ≥1 calendar week before cascade declares the adopter onboarded...' The phrase 'each cascade slice satisfies' followed by unconditional item (b) implies ALL cascade slices produce an invocation-log entry. The blocked-on-adopter-conflict close-state explicitly has no invocation-log entry — CI asserts the log file was NOT modified (invariant 2, §5.2, R-024-06). FIX-ROUND-4.md item 'R4-NEW-MAJ-002 (correctness)' fixed §3 line 73 to make the log-entry claim conditional on cascade-status being onboarded or onboarded-operator-bump and noted 'conflict-close path explicitly noted as no-log-entry.' The fix-round-4 inline-fix summary lists six edits, none of which touch §1. §1 item 2 remains unconditional. This is the same propagation-gap defect class documented in FIX-ROUND-4.md's 'Pattern observation': fix-round-3's conditional fix propagated to invariant 2 + §5.2 + §6 024B but not to R-024-06; fix-round-4's conditional fix propagated to §3 but not to §1. §1 is the WP-level summary of 'what each cascade slice satisfies' — it is the first normative reference a cascade-slice author reads when planning their slice. A Codex executor reading §1 item 2 as the slice contract checklist would add a log entry to a blocked-on-adopter-conflict slice, causing CI to fail (because CI now asserts log NOT modified). The inconsistency creates a direct executor error path: the executor follows §1's checklist, produces a log entry, and the slice fails the CI check that §1 did not warn them about. Fix required: §1 item 2 item (b) should be conditioned on cascade-status being onboarded or onboarded-operator-bump, with a parenthetical noting that blocked-on-adopter-conflict slices do NOT produce a log entry (mirroring the fix applied to §3 at fix-round-4). This is the minimal one-sentence addition.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r5.json:27:      \"recommendation\": \"Amend §1 item 2 item (b) to: '(b) scripts/enable-required-check.sh invocation per D-035 with invocation-log entry under docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md — only when cascade-status is onboarded or onboarded-operator-bump; blocked-on-adopter-conflict slices produce no log entry (CI asserts log-file NOT modified per invariant 2)'. This mirrors the identical fix applied to §3 line 73 at fix-round-4.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r5.json:56:      \"evidence\": \"Invariant 2 onboarded path: 'PR MUST modify branch-protection-log.md AND the modified entry's target repo string MUST match the DISPATCH-NOTE's target.' Both are positive assertions. There is no assertion 'MUST NOT have a TF-024X-conflict reference' or 'MUST NOT have a STATUS.md stub row.' A slice could theoretically declare onboarded AND also file a conflict TF. However: (a) declaring onboarded while the log entry and target match is the success-path — no bypass incentive exists for a malicious actor here (the log entry proves the invocation occurred on the correct target); (b) the conflict TF reference check only fires under blocked-on-adopter-conflict path — no cross-contamination; (c) the bypass risk on the onboarded path is a false-target log entry (wrong target, forged content), mitigated by the target-match check. R4's scan (R4-SCAN-NEGATIVE-ASSERTION-GAP-COMPLETENESS) correctly identified that the onboarded path has no negative assertion to add. No new finding.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r2.json:11:      \"evidence\": \"Plan-doc §2 invariant 2 now reads: 'CI enforcement (lands in 024B as scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh): every cascade-slice PR that targets one of the cohort adopters MUST modify docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md AND the modified entry's target repo string MUST match the DISPATCH-NOTE's target. The check is a hard predecessor for slice 024C — 024C does not open until the enforcement script is committed + green.' §6 024B acceptance criteria row updated: 'check-invocation-log-entry.sh rejects PRs missing log entry / target mismatch; D-044 filed. All three components are hard predecessors for slice 024C.' FIX-ROUND-1.md claim matches the plan-doc text.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r2.json:17:      \"title\": \"Log path corrected throughout plan-doc to docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md (D-035-canonical path)\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r2.json:18:      \"evidence\": \"All occurrences of the log path in the plan-doc now read docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md: §1 item (2), §2 invariant 2, §3 (implementation slices dispatch contract), §5.2 step (2), §5.6 Threshold A criteria row 3, §8 Threshold A row 2. The branch-protection-log.md file at docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md contains the comment 'Estate cascade rollout = WP-SCP-024' confirming it is the D-035-canonical estate-cascade audit trail for WP-SCP-024 entries. The enable-required-check.sh script line 436 already hardcodes LOG_FILE='docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md' — the plan-doc path now matches the script path. FIX-ROUND-1.md claim that 'no script change needed' is correct. COR-MAJ-001 co-closure confirmed.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r2.json:53:      \"evidence\": \"Invariant 2 CI enforcement clause (added by MAJ-SAFE-001 fix): 'every cascade-slice PR that targets one of the cohort adopters MUST modify docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md AND the modified entry's target repo string MUST match the DISPATCH-NOTE's target.' Invariant 10 conflict resolution path (added by MIN-SAFE-006 fix): when a workflow name conflict is discovered, the cascade slice CLOSES on the SCP side with status blocked-on-adopter-conflict. In this path: (a) the adopter wrapper PR was either never opened or never merged, (b) enable-required-check.sh was never invoked (since the safety check in §5.4 requires the wrapper to have green-CI'd at least once before script invocation), therefore (c) there is no branch-protection log entry to commit. Invariant 2's CI check (check-invocation-log-entry.sh) will either: (a) fail the conflict-close PR because the log file was not modified — blocking a legitimate close of a conflict-blocked cascade slice, or (b) be silently inapplicable to conflict-close PRs via an undocumented carve-out — creating an unspecified bypass path where a cascade-slice PR can merge without the log-modification gate being enforced. The plan-doc does not specify which outcome is correct. D-044 at 024B is supposed to 'ratify invariants 2 + 3 + 10 in code form' but the conflict between invariant 2 and invariant 10's conflict-close path is unresolved at plan-doc level — leaving it to the 024B implementer to discover the tension at implementation time, with no spec guidance on resolution.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-safety-r2.json:70:      \"recommendation\": \"Specify in §9 D-044 or §5.2 that forward-filed TFs for Renovate-disabled adopters MUST be recorded as a named entry in the invocation log under docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md (or a parallel WP-SCP-024 TF registry file), so the Threshold A reviewer at 024G has a machine-readable audit trail. Alternatively, specify that the TF-024X-renovate-<adopter> entry is filed in STATUS.md with an explicit closure criterion, consistent with how WP-SCP-022 tracked its TFs.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-completeness-r6.json:7:  \"summary\": \"All 6 R5 findings (R5-NEW-MAJ-001, R5-NEW-MAJ-002 ×3 lenses, R5-NEW-MIN-001, R5-NEW-nit-001) are genuinely closed. The fix-round-5 companion sweep closures (§5.6 branch-protection-log clarification + §8 blocked-on-adopter-conflict non-counting) are confirmed landed. However, the R5 safety MAJ-002 fix — which added the meaningful-content regex spec to invariant 2 — introduced two new MAJ that prevent fixpoint: (1) the regex scope header says 'STATUS.md list marker' but the blocked-on-adopter-conflict CI check targets DISPATCH-NOTE, creating an unspecified file/format requirement for the conflict-path TF reference; (2) the regex terminal clause '.{20,}' (any 20 characters including whitespace) contradicts the prose description '≥20 non-whitespace characters of description', leaving two different implementations possible for a Codex executor told to 'implement this regex literally'. A new MIN is also present: §5.6 first Threshold A bullet conflates the ≥3 counting criterion with the invocation-log-entries-committed requirement in a way that is inconsistent with §8's first bullet, which separates them. Fixpoint not reached.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-completeness-r6.json:14:      \"evidence\": \"Plan-doc §5.2 item (2) blocked-on-adopter-conflict bullet (line 130): 'a TF-024X-conflict-<adopter> TF is filed + referenced in DISPATCH-NOTE **with meaningful content per invariant 2's regex spec** (status field + ≥20 char description; bare-prefix stub fails CI); the PR MUST NOT modify branch-protection-log.md (CI script asserts absence of modification per invariant 2). Closes 024A R5 R5-NEW-MAJ-001 (completeness — §5.2 missing meaningful-content qualifier).' The meaningful-content qualifier is now present with an explicit cross-reference to invariant 2's regex spec. The closes marker is present and correctly lens-qualified. R5-NEW-MAJ-001 is CLOSED.\"\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-completeness-r6.json:21:      \"evidence\": \"Plan-doc R-024-06 mitigation (line 219): 'check-invocation-log-entry.sh (a) verifies the TF-024X-conflict-<adopter> reference IS present in DISPATCH-NOTE **with meaningful content matching invariant 2's regex spec** (status field + ≥20 char description; bare-prefix stubs fail CI) AND (b) actively asserts that branch-protection-log.md was NOT modified in the PR diff... All three checks are required; positive evidence + meaningful-content format match + negative assertion. Closes 024A R4 R4-NEW-MAJ-001 (R-024-06 stale language flagged by all 3 lenses) + 024A R5 R5-NEW-MAJ-002 (completeness — meaningful-content qualifier).' The meaningful-content qualifier is now present in assertion (a); the closes marker for R5-NEW-MAJ-002 completeness is present. R5-NEW-MAJ-002 (completeness) is CLOSED.\"\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-completeness-r6.json:56:      \"companion_finding\": \"§5.6 Threshold A telemetry — branch-protection-log.md entry counting clarification (logged per FIX-ROUND-5.md as '§6 line 176' companion fix)\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-completeness-r6.json:58:      \"evidence\": \"Plan-doc §5.6 (line 187): 'docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md is current (one entry per adopter that closed with cascade-status: onboarded or cascade-status: onboarded-operator-bump; conflict-close slices have NO entry per invariant 2's negative assertion).' The companion fix distinguishing onboarded/onboarded-operator-bump entries from conflict-close (no-entry) slices is present. Closed.\"\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-completeness-r6.json:65:      \"evidence\": \"Plan-doc §8 (line 239): 'Each adopter that reached cascade-status: onboarded or cascade-status: onboarded-operator-bump has a corresponding entry in docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md... Adopters whose cascade slice closed with cascade-status: blocked-on-adopter-conflict have NO log entry (per invariant 2's negative assertion) and do NOT count toward Threshold A's \\\"≥3 of 5\\\" criterion until a successor sub-slice onboards them per invariant 10.' The companion fix clarification is present. Closed.\"\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-completeness-r6.json:199:      \"evidence\": \"Plan-doc §5.6 (line 185): '≥3 of the 5 cohort adopters have `policy-check / scp/policy-check` required-check live + invocation log entries committed.' This formulation makes 'invocation log entries committed' a necessary condition for an adopter to count toward the ≥3 Threshold A criterion. Plan-doc §8 (line 238) formulates the same counting criterion as: '≥3 of {PIM, recommender, mapp-doc-agent, control-tower, shopify-app} have `policy-check / scp/policy-check` as a required status check on default branch.' In §8, the log-entry requirement is a SEPARATE criterion in the following bullet (line 239): 'Each adopter that reached cascade-status: onboarded or cascade-status: onboarded-operator-bump has a corresponding entry in docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md...' The formulations are inconsistent: §5.6 counts only adopters with BOTH check-live AND log-entry toward ≥3; §8 counts adopters with check-live toward ≥3 and treats log-entry as a separate completeness requirement. In practice they are operationally equivalent (every check-live adopter via onboarded/onboarded-operator-bump paths has a log entry), but the formal specification is inconsistent. A reviewer using §5.6 as the Threshold A criterion would reach a different conclusion about whether log-entry absence precludes an adopter from the ≥3 count than a reviewer using §8. The severity is MIN rather than MAJ because: (a) the inconsistency has no practical operational impact (the paths are coupled); (b) this is a pre-existing inconsistency that was present before R5 and not introduced by any R5 fix (the companion sweep fixed §5.6 third bullet and §8 second bullet, not the first bullets of each). Fix-round-6 should align §5.6 first bullet with §8's cleaner formulation.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-completeness-r6.json:200:      \"recommendation\": \"Update plan-doc §5.6 first Threshold A criterion (line 185) to align with §8's formulation. Replace '≥3 of the 5 cohort adopters have `policy-check / scp/policy-check` required-check live + invocation log entries committed' with '≥3 of the 5 cohort adopters have `policy-check / scp/policy-check` as a required status check on their default branch (only adopters whose cascade slice closed with cascade-status: onboarded or onboarded-operator-bump count; blocked-on-adopter-conflict adopters do NOT count toward this criterion until a successor sub-slice onboards them)'. The log-entry requirement for these adopters is already specified in §5.6 third bullet ('branch-protection-log.md is current (one entry per adopter that closed with cascade-status: onboarded or cascade-status: onboarded-operator-bump; conflict-close slices have NO entry per invariant 2's negative assertion)'); removing it from the first bullet eliminates the conflation. Add a closes marker: 'Closes 024A R6 R6-NEW-MIN-001 (§5.6 vs §8 Threshold A first criterion inconsistency)'.\"\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/dispatches/024A-plan-doc/review-correctness-r8.json:55:      \"evidence\": \"**§5.6 vs §8 Threshold A criteria comparison:**\\n§5.6 (lines 190-194) lists 5 bullets; §8 (lines 243-246) lists 4 mandatory bullets.\\n\\nMapping of §5.6 to §8:\\n- §5.6 bullet 1 ↔ §8 bullet 1: ≥3 of 5 with required-check. §5.6 includes cascade-status qualifier inline; §8 distributes the qualifier to bullet 2. Functionally equivalent.\\n- §5.6 bullet 2 ↔ §8 bullet 3: Renovate version-bump criterion. See R8-NEW-MIN-001 for a minor phrasing gap at this bullet.\\n- §5.6 bullet 3 ↔ §8 bullet 2: branch-protection-log currency (one entry per onboarded adopter; conflict-close NO entry). Phrasing slightly different but substantively identical.\\n- §5.6 bullet 4 (scorecards opt-in-registry): informational / MAY criterion. Correctly absent from §8's mandatory criteria (§8 says '4 mandatory criteria'). CORRECT omission.\\n- §5.6 bullet 5 ↔ §8 bullet 4: USER-GATE-E signed. Identical.\\n\\nThe closes-marker at §5.6 line 189 ('phrased identically to §8 to avoid drift; closes 024A R6 R6-NEW-MIN-001 completeness') is approximately accurate: the 4 mandatory criteria are all present in §5.6, and R6-NEW-MIN-001 was about adding the §5.6 content to match §8's structure. The claim 'phrased identically' is imprecise (§5.6 has 5 bullets including one informational; §8 has 4 mandatory bullets), but this is a bookkeeping precision issue, not a substantive spec inconsistency.\\n\\n**Cohort order consistency:**\\n§1 (lines 29-31): PIM → control-tower → mapp-doc-agent + recommender (paired) → shopify-app. §5.1 (lines 115-119): same order. §6 slice table: 024C=PIM, 024D=CT, 024E=paired, 024F=shopify-app. All consistent.\\n\\n**Cascade-status path mixing:**\\nInvariant 2 defines 3 named paths + fail-closed default. §5.2 declares 3 values. §6 slice rows reference invariant 2 per cascade-status. §8 Threshold A correctly excludes blocked-on-adopter-conflict from the ≥3 count. R-024-07 correctly excludes operator-bump adopters from Threshold A's Renovate criterion. No path-mixing inconsistencies found.\\n\\n**Closes-marker chain integrity:**\\nR7-NEW-MAJ-001 (correctness) closes-markers appear at all three fixed loci (§5.2/R-024-06/R-024-07). R7-NEW-CRIT-001 closes-marker appears at invariant 2 regex line 55. R7-NEW-MAJ-002 closes-marker appears at DISPATCH-NOTE risk item 4. All close-marker references resolve to known findings in prior round JSON files.\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
